Language model performance degrades rapidly as context window size expands—the well-documented "lost in the middle" phenomenon. In complex software development workflows, passing thousands of lines of irrelevant codebase context into an agent node increases latency, token cost, and hallucination rates.

Thesmos (agent-spine) solves this through **Cognitive Knapsack Injection**.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Task["Incoming Task Intent"] --> Router["tekmor route_task<br/>(Semantic Embedding & Index)"]
    Router --> Knapsack["_brain_knapsack<br/>(~500 surgical tokens)"]
    Knapsack --> AgentNode["Thesmos Agent Node<br/>(Bounded Sub-Agent)"]
    AgentNode --> Verify["Verify Node<br/>(Deterministic Bash Execution)"]
```

## The Knapsack Mechanism

When a DAG reaches a `NodeKind::Agent` execution step:
1. Thesmos calls `tekmor route_task` with the step's prompt and intent.
2. `tekmor` uses local embeddings (`all-MiniLM-L6-v2`) and procedure graph transitions to extract precisely the required functions, interfaces, and past trajectories.
3. It packages these into a compact JSON object named `_brain_knapsack` (strictly constrained to under 500 tokens).
4. The worker agent receives only this surgical knapsack rather than an entire codebase dump.

## Example Injected Payload

```json
{
  "_brain_knapsack": {
    "target_symbol": "tekmor::receipt::compute_tree_hash",
    "relevant_types": ["Receipt", "Verdict", "TreeSha"],
    "procedure_edge": "reproduce_failure -> isolate_and_patch",
    "invariant": "Tree SHA must ignore *.md and .claude/ directory"
  }
}
```

By keeping context windows ultra-compact, agent worker turns run with higher accuracy, lower cost, and deterministic predictability.
